    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a>, the Chief Minister and general of the Suri ruler Adil Shah, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> on 7 October 1556 and claimed royal status as Vikramaditya, about a month before the battle.</li><li>His army was the larger one, with 500 armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, but a Mughal detachment had captured his artillery on the way, and he fought without guns.</li><li>The battle was fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a> seemed close to victory: a chance arrow struck him in the eye, he collapsed unconscious, and his army panicked.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a> had been victorious in 22 battles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> before Panipat, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> took 120 of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, whose rampages impressed them so much that elephants soon became an integral part of Mughal military strategy.</p></aside>
    </section>

