    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1540</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> drives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and establishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1555</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> defeat Sikandar Shah Suri and regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1556</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> dies, and Hemchandra marches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> against the territories the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> held</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1556</span><span class="rf-text">Hemchandra defeats the Mughal governor Tardi Beg Khan and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, claiming royal status as Vikramaditya</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1556</span><span class="rf-text">Ali Quli Khan Shaibani captures Hemchandra's artillery on the march towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1556</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> defeat Hemchandra's army at Panipat after he is struck by an arrow</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1556</span><span class="rf-text">Hemchandra is captured and beheaded, and his head and body are displayed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1557</span><span class="rf-text">Adil Shah is defeated and killed by Khizr Khan, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-muhammad-shah-ghazi">Muhammad Khan Sur</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="second-battle-of-panipat" data-v="c5a1e2a2b5" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies face each other at Panipat</b> The Mughals stood with Ali Quli Khan's cavalry in the centre and a wing on each side. Hemu, with the larger army, led from an elephant between his two wings.</li><li><b>Hemu looses his elephants on the Mughal wings</b> Hemu began the attack and sent his elephants among the Mughal right and left wings. The Mughal centre advanced and took up a position before a ravine that his elephants and horse could not cross.</li><li><b>Mughal horsemen turn on the elephants</b> Men who escaped the elephants veered to the sides and attacked the flanks of Hemu's cavalry with arrows. The Mughal cavalry cut at the elephants' legs and riders, and Hemu pulled his elephants back.</li><li><b>Ali Quli rides round to the Afghan rear</b> As the Afghan attack slackened, Ali Quli Khan led his cavalry out, circled round and fell on the Afghan centre from behind. Hemu hurried to meet this charge.</li><li><b>The Mughal wings are driven back</b> Hemu kept leading counterattacks, and both Mughal wings were driven back. He moved his elephants and cavalry forward to crush the Mughal centre.</li><li><b>Hemu is struck by an arrow and his army flees</b> Hemu was struck in the eye by a chance Mughal arrow and collapsed unconscious. His army panicked, broke formation and fled, and many were killed in flight.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/humayun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humayun.jpg" alt="Humayun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Humayun_seated_(made_in_Kabul_in_1550-1555).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Circa 1550-55 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>, the successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, lost his inheritance when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> chased him out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a> in 1540. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> fell into Sher Shah's hands, but he died in 1545 at Kalinjar. His younger son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-shah-suri">Islam Shah Suri</a>, was a capable ruler, but on his death in 1554 the Sur Empire was caught up in a succession battle and plagued by rebellion and the secession of provinces. Humayun used the discord to recapture what he had lost, and on 23 July 1555 the Mughals defeated Sikandar Shah Suri and regained control of Delhi and Agra.</p><p>Islam Shah's rightful successor, his 12-year-old son Firoz Khan, was murdered by his maternal uncle, who took the throne as Adil Shah Suri. The new ruler was more interested in pleasure than in the affairs of state, which were left mainly to Hemchandra, an old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> associate of Sher Shah Suri from Rewari. Hemchandra had risen from humble circumstances to become Adil Shah's Chief Minister and the general of the Suri army. He was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> when Humayun died on 27 January 1556, an opportunity to defeat the Mughals and reclaim lost territory.</p><p>Hemchandra marched rapidly from Bengal and drove the Mughals out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayana">Bayana</a>, Etawah, Bharthana, Bidhuna, Lakhna, Sambhal, Kalpi and Narnaul. The governor of Agra evacuated the city and fled without a fight. Hemchandra pursued him to Tughlaqabad, a village just outside Delhi, where he met and defeated the forces of the Mughal governor of Delhi, Tardi Beg Khan. He took possession of Delhi after a day's battle on 7 October 1556 and assumed the title of Vikramaditya.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Second Battle of Panipat&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>Jadunath Sarkar, Military History of India (Orient Longmans, 1960)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akbar.jpg" alt="Akbar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Govardhan._Akbar_With_Lion_and_Calf_ca._1630,_Metmuseum_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Govardhan / Mir Ali Heravi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mughal army was led by Ali Quli Khan Shaibani, later Khan-i-Zaman, with his 10,000 cavalry in the centre. Sikandar Khan Uzbak held the right and Abdulla Khan Uzbak the left. The vanguard was led by Husain Quli Beg and Shah Quli Mahram and included Bairam Khan's detachment of Turks. The 13-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, who had succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>, and his guardian Bairam Khan did not take part in the fighting: they stayed in the rear, eight miles from the battleground.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a>'s army was numerically superior. It included a 30,000-strong cavalry force of Afghan horse riders and an elephant contingent numbering 500, each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephant</a> protected by plate armour and mounted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> and crossbowmen. Hemchandra led his army himself, mounted on an elephant named Hawai. His left was led by his sister's son, Ramya, and the right by Shadi Khan Kakar. The army was experienced and confident, and Hemu had by then been victorious in 22 battles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><p>In this battle, however, Hemchandra had no artillery. On the march Ali Quli Khan, who had been sent ahead with a 10,000-strong cavalry force, came upon Hemchandra's artillery being transported under a weak guard. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> abandoned the guns and fled without making a stand, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> captured the entire train. The loss proved costly for Hemu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 5 November 1556 the Mughal army met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a>'s army on the historic battlefield of Panipat. Hemchandra began the attack himself and loosed his elephants against the right and left wings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. The soldiers who escaped the rampage did not retreat but veered to the sides and attacked the flanks of Hemu's cavalry, pelting them with their superior archery. The Mughal centre advanced and took up a defensive position before a deep ravine, which neither Hemchandra's elephants nor his horse could cross, leaving them vulnerable to the missiles fired from the other side.</p><p>Meanwhile the Mughal cavalry, on swift mounts, made inroads into the Afghan ranks from the flanks and the rear and began targeting the elephants, slashing at their legs or taking out their riders. Hemchandra pulled back his elephants and the Afghan attack relented. Seeing this, Ali Quli Khan led his cavalry out, circled round and fell on the Afghan centre from the rear. Hemu, watching from his howdah atop Hawai, hurried to counter the charge.</p><p>Even after Shadi Khan Kakar and another of his lieutenants, Bhagwan Das, went down, Hemu kept leading counterattacks, running down any who challenged his elephants. The battle was desperately contested but the advantage seemed to have tilted towards him. Both Mughal wings had been driven back, and he moved his elephants and cavalry forward to crush their centre. At this point, possibly on the cusp of victory, he was struck in the eye by a chance Mughal arrow and collapsed unconscious. His army, seeing him go down, panicked, broke formation and fled.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Second Battle of Panipat&#8221; cites where it covers Battle:</p><ul><li>Jadunath Sarkar, Military History of India (Orient Longmans, 1960)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was lost. Some 5,000 dead lay on the field, and many more were killed while fleeing. The elephant carrying the unconscious and almost dead Hemchandra was captured several hours after the fighting ended and led to the Mughal camp. Bairam Khan asked the 13-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> to behead him. Akbar is said by one account to have refused to take the sword to a dead man, while another says he followed Bairam Khan's advice and beheaded Hemchandra himself and took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>.</p><p>Hemchandra's head was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, to be hung outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> Darwaja, while his body was gibbeted on a gate of the Purana Quila in Delhi, where he had held his coronation on 6 October. Several of his supporters and relatives were also beheaded.</p><p>With Hemchandra gone, Adil Shah's fortunes took a turn for the worse. He was defeated and killed by Khizr Khan, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamsuddin-muhammad-shah-ghazi">Muhammad Khan Sur</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, in April 1557. The spoils of Panipat included 120 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak.jpg" alt="Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abul_Fazl_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Govardhan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Two writers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s court give the accounts of what became of Hemchandra. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak</a>, Akbar's later courtier, writes that Akbar refused to take his sword to a dead man. Muhammad Arif Qandhari, a contemporary who composed the Tarikh e Akbari, writes that Akbar followed Bairam Khan's advice and beheaded Hemchandra himself and took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>. The account of Akbar's refusal is probably a later invention of his courtiers.</p><p>The Akbarnama is also a source of images: a painting of the minaret raised after the battle is one of the 56 paintings of Akbar's life in the Akbarnama copy. Both writers served or wrote under the Mughal court, so the fate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a> is known from the victors' side.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat.jpg" alt="First Battle of Panipat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_battle_of_Panipat_and_the_death_of_Sultan_Ibr%C4%81h%C4%ABm,_the_last_of_the_L%C5%8Dd%C4%AB_Sultans_of_Delhi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ẓahīr ud-Dīn Muḥammad Bābur (1483-1530); It contains 30 mostly full-page miniatures in fine Mughal style by at least two different artists., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a>'s march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> had briefly put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> in his hands, and the battle was fought to take them back. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, who had regained the two cities in July 1555, held them after Panipat, and Adil Shah was himself defeated and killed within months.</p><p>The captured elephants left a mark on Mughal warfare. Their destructive rampages so impressed the Mughals that elephants soon became an integral part of Mughal military strategy. Hemu is also commemorated at Panipat: a memorial was erected at the spot where he was beheaded, and it is now known as Hemu's Samadhi Sthal.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, 13 years old at the time of the battle and kept eight miles from the fighting, is said to have taken the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> after it. The battle was fought not far from the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> of 1526.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 5 November 1556 the Mughal army of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and Bairam Khan defeated Hemchandra Vikramaditya at Panipat, a month after he had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. A chance arrow struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hemu</a> down as he seemed close to winning, his army fled, and he was captured and beheaded.</em></p>

